# Line endings are part of these files' correctness, not a checkout preference.
#
# POSIX shells refuse a script whose shebang line ends CRLF, and this kit is
# cloned onto Windows machines as well as Linux ones. Without this file, a clone
# on Windows (core.autocrlf=true) rewrites every text file, so a checkout there
# produces install.sh / start.sh / verify-upgrade.sh that fail on the Linux
# machines when the directory is copied or the clone is shared.
#
# Scripts that any shell must read get LF everywhere. So does the DATA the tools parse:
# `docs/adrs/*.adr.md`, `docs/specs/*.spec.md`, `plugins/inventory.json` and every
# plugin README/LICENSE. Those were left to the platform, and a Windows checkout then
# held CRLF in exactly them — which broke the panel's line-oriented parsers (`.` never
# matches `\r`, so a spec parsed to zero laws) and made the tarball-versus-source check
# fail on an unchanged kit. A file whose bytes are read by a program is not "natural to
# edit on Windows"; it is input.
#
# `.ps1` stays CRLF, because PowerShell is the one consumer that prefers it.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
LICENSE text eol=lf
NOTICE  text eol=lf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.tgz   binary
